{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"An ETF’s quoted spread reflects the cost and risk of providing immediacy—not just trading the ETF. If its holdings are costly or difficult to trade due to wide spreads, shallow depth, market closures, or volatility, market makers face greater hedging costs and uncertainty, so they may quote wider. Using correlated instruments can help when direct hedging is impractical, but adds basis and execution risk.\n\nCreation and redemption help align ETF prices with portfolio value, but basket trading, financing, fees, operational constraints, and inventory risk make arbitrage imperfect and can affect quotes.\n\nHistorical exchange volume measures past ETF trading, not current order-book depth, underlying liquidity, or dealers’ ability to hedge and process baskets. A lightly traded ETF can still have competitive quotes if its holdings and hedges are liquid; high volume alone doesn’t ensure tight spreads."}}
